      "The primary challenge to the Irenaean tradition of apostolic authorship comes from a fragment of Papias of Hierapolis, preserved in Eusebius's Ecclesiastical History. Richard Bauckham in particular has built his influential Elder John hypothesis upon this fragment, arguing that Papias distinguishes between two figures both named John — the apostle son of Zebedee and a separate John the Elder — and that it is this second John who authored the Fourth Gospel. While Bauckham's argument has gained considerable traction in contemporary Johannine scholarship, a careful reading of the primary text reveals that the two Johns hypothesis is not Papias's own claim but rather Eusebius's editorial inference imposed upon it.",
