      "It is against this theological backdrop that the eyewitness claim of 1:14 must be understood in its full weight: 'And the Word became flesh, and dwelt among us; and we saw His glory, glory as of the only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth.' Three things demand attention here.",
      "First the incarnational claim itself — 'the Word became flesh.' Ladd notes that 'such an affirmation would amaze and refute all Hellenistic philosophical and gnostic dualisms that separated God from this world,' and that the author 'wishes to emphasize that it was God himself in the Word who entered human history, not as a phantom, but as a real man of flesh.' The one who existed before creation, the agent through whom all things came into being, entered human history as a real historical person. This is not mythology — it is a claim about a specific historical event.",
      "Second the tabernacle allusion — 'dwelt among us.' The Greek word skenoo means literally to tabernacle, to pitch a tent. As Ladd observes this is 'a biblical metaphor for God's presence,' implying 'that God himself was present in the flesh, in abasement.' The author is deliberately invoking the Shekinah glory of God dwelling among Israel in the wilderness — the same glory that filled the tabernacle, the same presence that accompanied Israel through the desert, the same God who told Moses 'I will dwell among the children of Israel' has now pitched his tent in human flesh. The theological continuity with the Old Testament is total and intentional.",
